## Final Judgement
The key insight from the fact-check is that the evidence clearly shows Joe Biden did not utter the exact phrase "If Your 8-Year-Old Child Says They Want To Be Transgender, They Have A Right To Transition." Instead, he responded to a mother's question during an ABC town hall on October 15, 2020, by stating that an 8- or 10-year-old child can decide they want to be transgender and that "there should be zero discrimination." The sources consistently quote Biden's actual words, which do not include any mention of a "right to transition." The claim is presented as a direct quote, but no primary source or transcript supports that phrasing; the Federalist article paraphrases his remarks but does not provide the exact wording. No reputable fact-checking organization confirms the claim, and the context makes clear that Biden was advocating against discrimination, not endorsing an unconditional right to transition for a child.

Since the evidence explicitly and clearly refutes the claim—Joe Biden never said the words attributed to him—the decision is that the claim is `refuted`.

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
The fact-check confirms that Joe Biden never said the exact phrase attributed to him. During an ABC town hall on October 15, 2020, Biden responded to a mother’s question by stating that an 8- or 10-year-old child can decide they want to be transgender and that “there should be zero discrimination” ([Yahoo Finance](https://finance.yahoo.com/news/joe-biden-promises-mom-transgender-170710065.html)). No primary source or transcript supports the wording “right to transition”; the Federalist article paraphrases his remarks but does not provide the exact quote ([The Federalist](https://thefederalist.com/2020/10/19/joe-biden-tells-mom-he-supports-transgendering-eight-year-old-kids/)). No reputable fact-checking organization has verified the claimed phrase, and the context shows Biden was advocating against discrimination, not endorsing an unconditional right to transition. Therefore, the claim is refuted.